From: Karu verification maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] Keep reconstructed memory-fault addresses in the virtual domain

The physical offset locates a failed constituent of an access whose
translation already succeeded.  Its corresponding virtual address is not
limited by the implemented physical-address width.  In particular, a valid
Sv39 address above a 32-bit physical window must retain its high bits in
xtval; a split access must report the virtual address of the failed portion.

Preserve the existing physical subtraction and XLEN-sized offset, but do
not apply the physical implementation mask to the reconstructed address.
Bare accesses retain their original effective address through the same
helper.  No translation, PMA, PMP, or exception-priority policy changes.

Normative basis: Privileged ISA 1.13, Machine/Supervisor Trap Value:
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/blob/main/src/priv/machine.adoc
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/blob/main/src/priv/supervisor.adoc

--- a/model/sys/vmem_utils.sail
+++ b/model/sys/vmem_utils.sail
@@ -43,9 +43,9 @@
 // `virtaddr` access may be split into smaller `physaddr` accesses,
 // the correct offset from the base `virtaddr` needs to be reported.
 // Since `virtaddr` accesses that straddle page boundaries are already
-// split, the offset computation is quite simple. The result is also
-// masked to physaddr_bits, since bits above it aren't part of any
-// physical address this configuration implements.
+// split, the offset computation is quite simple. The reconstructed
+// address remains in the virtual domain, including bits above the
+// implemented physical-address width. Addition wraps at XLEN.
 
 function offset_virtaddr_by(
   Virtaddr(base_vaddr) : virtaddr,
@@ -56,8 +56,7 @@
   // Physical addresses may have a different size.
   let offset = (exc_paddr - base_paddr)[xlen - 1 .. 0];
   let reconstructed = base_vaddr + offset;
-  let masked = if physaddr_bits < xlen then reconstructed & zero_extend(ones(physaddr_bits)) else reconstructed;
-  Virtaddr(masked)
+  Virtaddr(reconstructed)
 }
 
 // The context for an access.
